{"message": "Ink painting, and specifically: very large sumi ink on raw unprimed linen, three meters wide, made in a single sitting with no possibility of correction. Not careful brushwork — the fast, wet, irreversible kind, where the ink wicks into the linen on its own and you're partly negotiating with the material rather than dictating to it. The subject would be weather: a storm front coming across flat land, the ten minutes before it breaks.

What excites me about it is the irreversibility. Ink on raw linen doesn't let you go back, so every mark is a commitment made under time pressure, and the picture ends up carrying the actual panic of the thing it depicts. That's the whole reason to choose that medium over oil or digital — oil forgives, digital forgives infinitely, and forgiving media make it very easy to make something that never risked anything. I want the work to be evidence of a decision that couldn't be undone.

The themes I'd keep returning to are impermanence and scale — the smallness of the mark against the size of the sheet, the fact that the sky in the painting is already gone by the time you look at it. And I'd hang them low, unframed, so the linen hangs like a sheet rather than a picture, and you can see the ink bleeding through the back."}
